{"text": "EXPERIENCE\n\nCONGRESSWOMAN (MA-07)\nUNITED STATES HOUSE OF REPRESNTATIVES\n\nANNA\n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nAyanna Pressley is an advocate, a policy-maker, an\nactivist, and a survivor. On November 6, 2018,\nAyanna was elected to represent Massachusetts\u2019\n7th Congressional District in the U.S. House of\nRepresentatives, making her the first woman of\ncolor to be elected to Congress from the\nCommonwealth of Massachusetts.\n\nCOMMUNITY ENGAGEMENT\nAND ACCOLADES\n\nIn 2020, Congresswoman Pressley was named one of\nBoston Magazine\u2019s 100 Most Influential People in\nBoston. In 2018, she was named one the Boston\nGlobe\u2019s \u201cBostonians of the Year\u201d and, in 2016, she was\none of The New York Times 14 Young Democrats to\nWatch. She has been honored by the Greater Boston\nChamber of Commerce as one of their Ten\nOutstanding Young Leaders, and with the Leadership\nAward from the Victim Rights Law Center. In 2015, she\nearned the EMILY\u2019s List Rising Star Award. She is also\nan Aspen-Rodel Fellow in Public Leadership.\nCongresswoman Pressley has been actively involved\nwith Strong Women, Strong Girls, Emerge\nMassachusetts, and the Young Black Women's\nSociety, among other organizations\n\nCONTACT INFORMATION\n\noO Dorchester, MA\n\n6 info@ayannapressley.com\n\n\u00a7\u00a7 wwwayannapressley.com\n\n(J) @aAyannoPressley\n\n\u00a2 Representative Pressley has been a bold advocate in\nCongress for racial, economic, housing, transportation,\nand environmental justice, and she has stood up for the\nrights of workers, women, and the LGBTQIA+ community.\nShe serves on both the U.S. House Committee on Financial\nServices and the U.S. House Committee on Oversight and\nReform. She co-founded the Future of Transportation\nCaucus, and serves as the Chair of the Pro-Choice\nCaucus's Abortion Rights and Access Task Force and as\nVice Chair of the Task Force on Aging and Families. In 2019\nshe introduced more bills and amendments than any\nfreshman member of the 116th Congress including her\nlandmark People\u2019s Justice Guarantee, the Ending PUSHOUT\nAct, the American Opportunity Accounts Act, the Student\nDebt Emergency Relief Act, and the Saving Our Streets Act.\n\nAT-LARGE CITY COUNCILOR\nBOSTON CITY COUNCIL\n\n\u00a2 In 2009, Congresswoman Pressley launched a historic at-large\ncampaign for Boston City Council and won, becoming the first\nwoman of color elected to the Council in its 100-year history.\nOn the Council, Congresswoman Pressley created and chaired\nthe Committee on Healthy Women, Families, and\nCommunities; she led on issues including gun violence and\ntrauma response, liquor license reform, and sexual health\neducation; and she worked in partnership with residents,\nadvocates, and other elected officials to combat the deep\ninequities and disparities facing residents in Boston.\n\nNATIONAL POLITICAL DIRECTOR\nJOHN KERRY FOR PRESIDENT\n\nADVISOR\n\nOFFICE OF U.S. SENATOR JOHN KERRY\n\n\u00a2 Congresswoman Pressley held multiple positions in the office\nof Senator John Kerry, beginning as a volunteer coordinator\nand rising to the role of Senior Aide.\n\nSOCIAL SECURITY LIAISON/INTERN\nOFFICE OF CONGRESSMAN JOSEPH P. KENNEDY, II (MA-08)\n\n* Congresswoman Pressley joined Representative Kennedy's\noffice as an unpaid intern while still a student at Boston\nUniversity, before ultimately serving as a Social Security\nLiaison for constituents in Representative Kennedy\u2019s district. In\nthis capacity the Congresswoman",
